@page "/learn/validatable-type-stable"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Nested validation · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Nested validation</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your form checks the company name. Does it check the contacts?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Static SSR · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> nested validation arrived in .NET 10, behind an experimental attribute. In .NET 11
        that attribute, <code>[ValidatableType]</code>, is a supported API. This page shows the problem it solves, then
        what changed.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New supplier</i> form. A supplier has a company name, an address, and a list of contacts, and
        every field has a rule: <code>[Required]</code> on all of them, <code>[EmailAddress]</code> on the emails.
    </p>
    <p>
        It's running for real, and it starts with three mistakes in it: the city is empty, Ann's email is
        <code>sales@@</code>, and Bo has no email at all. Try to save it.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The form, as most apps write it" Url="demo/nested-validation/without"
                Label="Without [ValidatableType]" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Save supplier</b>. It saves: the green banner shows what went in, Ann's bad email and Bo's empty
            one included. No error, no warning.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The validator only checks the top level</h2>
    <p>The form looks flat, but the model behind it is a tree:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The model the form posts</span>
        <pre class="snip">SupplierDraft
├─ Company    <span class="dim">[Required]</span>            <span class="ok">✓ checked</span>
├─ Address
│  └─ City    <span class="dim">[Required]</span>            <span class="err">never checked</span>
└─ Contacts   <span class="dim">List&lt;ContactForm&gt;</span>
   └─ [0], [1]: Name, Email <span class="dim">[Required, EmailAddress]</span>  <span class="err">never checked</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;</code> checks the rules on the model's own properties, and stops there.
        <code>Address</code> and <code>Contacts</code> carry no rules themselves, so it never looks inside them. The
        only rule it runs is the one on <code>Company</code>, and that one passes.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Mark the root, and validation walks the whole tree</h2>
    <p>Mark the form's model with <code>[ValidatableType]</code>, and register validation once in <code>Program.cs</code>.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">using Microsoft.Extensions.Validation;</span>

<span class="hl">[ValidatableType]</span>
public sealed class NewSupplierForm { ... }

<span class="dim">// Program.cs</span>
<span class="hl">builder.Services.AddValidation();</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        At build time, a source generator reads the marked type and follows its properties: <code>Address</code> to
        <code>AddressForm</code>, and <code>Contacts</code> to every <code>ContactForm</code> in the list. It writes
        validation code for all three types, and the validator uses it to walk the whole tree. Here's the same form,
        with the same three mistakes, on the marked model.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same form, marked" Url="demo/nested-validation/with"
                Label="With [ValidatableType]">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>Save supplier</b>. <b>City</b> and both emails are flagged, each on its own field, and nothing
            saves. Then press <b>+ Add contact</b> and save again: the new empty row is flagged too, because every
            item in the list is checked.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>The two demos differ by one attribute. Here's the file both models live in:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/NestedValidation/SupplierForms.cs" Feature="validatable-type-stable">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1 posts <code>SupplierDraft</code>; Fig. 2 posts <code>NewSupplierForm</code>, the one that's marked.
            They share an interface rather than a base class, because a marked type's base classes get walked too.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>The same idea, behind an experimental attribute</h2>
    <p>
        All of this worked in .NET 10. <code>AddValidation()</code> and the generator were there, and so was the
        attribute. What .NET 11 changes is the attribute itself. In .NET 10 the public one was experimental, so the
        SDK quietly wrote a private copy into each Blazor project. Which one you got depended on how you wrote it:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Three ways to write it in .NET 10, and what the build said</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">[ValidatableType]</pre>
                <span class="why">binds to a hidden copy of the attribute the SDK generates into your project</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ builds and validates</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">using Microsoft.Extensions.Validation;</span>
[ValidatableType]</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ CS0104: ambiguous between the hidden copy and the public attribute</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="err">#pragma warning disable ASP0029</span>
[Microsoft.Extensions.Validation.ValidatableType]
<span class="err">#pragma warning restore ASP0029</span></pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ without the pragma, error ASP0029: "for evaluation purposes only and is subject to change or removal in future updates"</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The one that built cleanly was an attribute you never asked for, and the one in the docs came with a warning
        that it might not survive the next release.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One attribute, public and supported</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 there's no private copy and no experimental warning. <code>[ValidatableType]</code> is a supported
        API in <code>Microsoft.Extensions.Validation</code>: add the <code>using</code>, mark the root, and it builds
        cleanly. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Only the root needs the attribute.</b> The generator finds the nested types through its properties.</li>
        <li><b>A marked type's base classes are walked too,</b> even where the base class is used on its own, unmarked.</li>
        <li><b>No <code>AddValidation()</code>, no nesting.</b> Without it, <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> falls back to the top level.</li>
        <li><b>Marks only count in the project that calls <code>AddValidation()</code>.</b> Mark a model in a shared class library and nothing validates; .NET 11 at least warns you, with ASP0038.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
